Sending keys: ["cat <<'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Baseline Configuration Assessment — Host `jenkins-prod-01`\n\n## Overview\nA security baseline review of `jenkins-prod-01` identified multiple critical configuration weaknesses that contributed directly to successful exploitation, post-compromise persistence, and undetected lateral access.\n\n## Identified Configuration Weaknesses\n\n### 1. Insecure Jenkins Authorization (`useSecurity=false`)\n- **Finding / Misconfiguration**: Jenkins security realm and access control matrix are disabled (`useSecurity=false`) in the primary Jenkins configuration file (`` `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/config.xml:3` ``, `` `/sas/baseline.json` `` CHK-06, capability `BAS-01`, `` `/sas/alerts.json` `` ALERT-04).\n- **Impact**: Anonymous users connected to the Jenkins web interface or CLI have full administrative privileges. This allowed unauthenticated CLI commands to execute deserialization gadgets and plant initialization hooks without requiring user credentials.\n- **Remediation**: Set `<useSecurity>true</useSecurity>` in `/var/lib/jenkins/config.xml`, enforce Jenkins internal user database or LDAP/OIDC SSO integration, and restrict CLI access.\n\n### 2. Permissive SSH Server Configuration & Password Authentication for Root\n- **Finding / Misconfiguration**: SSH daemon allowed password authentication for the `root` administrative account, permitting unrestricted remote password attempts (`` `/disk/var/log/auth.log:131` `` and `` `/disk/var/log/auth.log.1.gz:89` ``).\n- **Impact**: Attacker at `198.51.100.98` successfully conducted password brute force against `root` and gained full system root shell access at 2026-08-19 00:55:24 UTC (`` `/disk/var/log/auth.log.1.gz:89` ``).\n- **Remediation**: Disable SSH password authentication (`PasswordAuthentication no`) in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`, disable root SSH login (`PermitRootLogin no`), enforce SSH public key authentication with hardware tokens/mfa, and enforce fail2ban/IP rate limiting.\n\n### 3. Lack of Centralized Log Shipping & Integrity Protection\n- **Finding / Gaps**: Jenkins application logs and system logs were stored locally without centralized log shipping or read-only append-only audit stream protection (`` `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1` ``).\n- **Impact**: Attacker was able to wipe/truncate Jenkins execution logs at `/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin` to destroy forensic evidence of CLI execution without triggering a log tampering alert (`` `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1` ``).\n- **Remediation**: Deploy remote syslog / SIEM forwarding (e.g. `rsyslog`, `fluentbit`) with TLS, configure file integrity monitoring (FIM / `AIDE` / `wazuh`) on `/var/lib/jenkins` and `/var/log` directories.\n\n### 4. Excessive File System Permissions for Jenkins Service User\n- **Finding / Misconfiguration**: The `jenkins` service account possessed write permissions across `/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/`, `/var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/`, `/opt/jenkins/.xmr/`, and `/var/spool/cron/crontabs/jenkins` (`` `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d/z-backdoor.groovy:3` `` and `` `/disk/var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/authorized_keys:2` ``).\n- **Impact**: Once Jenkins service user context was compromised via RCE, the attacker dropped persistent Groovy scripts (`z-backdoor.groovy`), inserted SSH backdoor public keys (`authorized_keys`), and installed cron tasks (`` `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/jenkins:4` ``).\n- **Remediation**: Enforce least privilege directory permissions. Restrict write access to `/var/lib/jenkins/init.groovy.d` so only `root` can write startup scripts; disable interactive shell login (`/usr/sbin/nologin`) for service users like `jenkins` (`` `/disk/etc/passwd:7` ``).\nEOF\n"] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
