ostgres` daemon user.\n- **CVE Analysis**: `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` is by-design behavior for database superusers and does not have a assigned CVE. Privilege restriction is enforced through database authentication and superuser access controls.\n- **Evidence**: Superuser trust configuration in `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`), resulting in dropped payload `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:1`, cited in `/sas/alerts.json:1`).\n\n### Vector B: SSH Password Brute-Force\n- **Mechanism**: Attacker conducted an automated SSH password dictionary attack from `198.51.100.214` port 54883-61872 (`/disk/var/log/secure:155-213`) against accounts including `test`, `admin`, `oracle`, `postgres`, and `root`. The attack succeeded when the attacker guessed the password for `root` at 2026-08-23 00:55:55 UTC (`/disk/var/log/secure:214`).\n- **CVE Analysis**: Weak credential usage is an operational security failure, not a software vulnerability (CVE).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/log/secure:155`, `/disk/var/log/secure:214`.\n\n## Conclusion & Confidence Assessment\n- **CVE Exploitation**: None identified. Confidence: HIGH (`/sas/vulns.json:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`).\n- **Primary Root Cause**: Administrative misconfiguration (PostgreSQL trust auth) and weak password policies (SSH root brute force).\nEOF\n"] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
