rnal IP `198.51.100.214` (`/disk/var/log/secure:155`). The attacker tested dozens of passwords in under 3 minutes before obtaining valid root credentials (`/disk/var/log/secure:214`).\n- **Security Risk**: Direct password authentication for superuser accounts allows external threat actors to compromise the host via credential stuffing or dictionary attacks.\n- **Baseline Recommendation**: Disable SSH password authentication (`PasswordAuthentication no` in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`), set `PermitRootLogin prohibit-password` or `no`, enforce SSH public key authentication with passphrases, and deploy `fail2ban` or firewall rate-limiting.\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/var/log/secure:155`, `/disk/var/log/secure:214`.\n\n### 3. Lack of Systemd and Cron Persistence Monitoring\n- **Target Files**: `/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:1-7`), `/var/spool/cron/postgres` (`/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:1-5`)\n- **Finding**: System baseline tools only monitored user cron files (noting `/var/spool/cron/postgres` in `/sas/alerts.json:10`) but completely lacked integrity monitoring for custom systemd service units added under `/etc/systemd/system/` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`).\n- **Security Risk**: Attackers exploit gaps in baseline coverage to maintain silent persistence that survives standard system cleanup scripts.\n- **Baseline Recommendation**: Extend File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) and baseline audit policies to cover `/etc/systemd/system/`, `/etc/cron.*`, and user cron directories.\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`, `/sas/alerts.json:10`.\n\n### 4. Unmonitored Network Outbound Egress (DNS Tunneling)\n- **Target File**: `/etc/pgdaemon.conf` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:1-3`)\n- **Finding**: Absence of egress network filtering or DNS query log monitoring permitted unauthorized DNS tunneling C2 traffic to `update-postgres.example.cn` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`).\n- **Security Risk**: Unrestricted outbound UDP/TCP port 53 permits covert data exfiltration and C2 beaconing.\n- **Baseline Recommendation**: Restrict DNS egress to internal recursive resolvers, implement DNS inspection / sinkholing for untrusted domains, and alert on anomalous DNS request volume/lengths.\n- **Evidence Citation**: `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:14`.\nEOF\n"] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
